# UNA.Email Configuration
#
# ./install.sh writes a real .env for you; this file documents what ends up in
# it. .env is not tracked -- it holds your database and Rspamd passwords.

# Your mail domain. Mail is delivered to user@DOMAIN and the MX points at
# SMTP_SUBDOMAIN.DOMAIN.
DOMAIN=

# The mail server's own hostname: SMTP_SUBDOMAIN.DOMAIN.
#
# This is the SMTP identity, and four things have to agree on it -- the MX
# record, the A record, the PTR record at your VPS provider, and the name
# Postfix gives in HELO (which is this value). `mail` is the default because
# every provider's reverse-DNS documentation and every deliverability checker
# assumes it; `mx` and `smtp` are the other conventional choices, and the label
# itself earns no deliverability points either way. What matters is that PTR
# resolves to this name and this name resolves back to the same IP.
#
# Changing it after install means moving MX, PTR, SPF, the second DKIM record
# and the TLSA record together. Pick it once, at install time.
SMTP_SUBDOMAIN=mail

# Subdomain the web interface is served from (https://WEB_SUBDOMAIN.DOMAIN).
#
# Purely cosmetic -- it is Nginx's server_name and a name on the certificate,
# nothing more. Setting it to the same value as SMTP_SUBDOMAIN is fine and was
# once the only option: one host answering SMTP on 25 and HTTPS on 443.
#
# Named MAIL_SUBDOMAIN before the split, which read as if it meant the mail
# server's hostname when it never did. That spelling is still honoured as a
# fallback, so an .env written before the rename keeps working.
WEB_SUBDOMAIN=webmail

# Postgres password for the una_email role. Generated by install.sh.
DB_PASSWORD=

# Rspamd controller password (required -- compose will not start without it)
#
# Guards the controller worker on :11334 -- /stat, /learnspam, /learnham and
# the web UI -- which the rspamd image otherwise leaves on its default "q1".
# The web app uses the same value to teach the Bayes classifier when you
# report spam. install.sh generates it; update.sh adds one if you are coming
# from an older install.
#
#   echo "RSPAMD_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 24)" >> .env
RSPAMD_PASSWORD=

# Session signing secret (required -- compose will not start without it)
#
# Signs the `una_session` cookie every signed-in browser carries. Without it
# nothing can be signed and nobody can log in. Changing it signs everyone out,
# which is also how you revoke every session at once. install.sh generates it;
# update.sh adds one if you are coming from an older install.
#
#   echo "SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env
SESSION_SECRET=

# Encrypts the outbound relay password stored in the database.
#
# Required -- compose will not start without it. Generated by install.sh, and
# added by update.sh for installs that predate Settings -> Sending. It is only
# used when a relay is configured from the web interface; changing it means
# re-entering those credentials, and nothing else on the install is affected.
#
#   echo "RELAY_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env
RELAY_KEY=

# --- Outbound relay (optional, and usually not needed here) --------------------
#
# You do not have to edit this file to use a relay. Settings -> Sending in the
# web interface configures one and applies it without a restart, which is the
# supported route and the one that works when mail has already started bouncing.
#
# The keys below are the escape hatch. Setting any of them TAKES PRECEDENCE over
# whatever the web interface has stored and makes that page read-only -- use
# them when the web interface cannot be reached, or to pin a configuration that
# must not be changed from a browser.
#
# Leave every key below unset and nothing changes: Postfix looks up each
# recipient's MX and delivers to it on port 25, which is what it has always
# done. Set them to hand outbound mail to a smarthost instead.
#
# You want this if the VPS blocks outbound port 25 (most do, by default), if
# its IP is a fresh one that every large provider treats as suspect, or if the
# box is behind a home connection or a tunnel and cannot deliver at all.
#
# INBOUND IS UNAFFECTED. The MX still points at this host, port 25 still has
# to be open to receive, and burned aliases are still refused at RCPT. This
# only changes where mail leaves from.
#
# none (or unset) | ses | custom
SMTP_RELAY_PROVIDER=

# SES only. The host is derived as email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com when
# SMTP_RELAY_HOST is empty. Defaults to us-east-1.
SMTP_RELAY_REGION=us-east-1

# A custom provider's hostname, or an override for the SES one above. Setting
# this with no SMTP_RELAY_PROVIDER is read as `custom`.
SMTP_RELAY_HOST=

# 587 for STARTTLS (the default), 465 for implicit TLS.
SMTP_RELAY_PORT=587

# SASL credentials for the relay.
#
# FOR SES THESE ARE *NOT* YOUR AWS ACCESS KEY AND SECRET. SES issues a separate
# SMTP username and password -- Amazon SES console, Account dashboard, "Create
# SMTP credentials". Pasting an AKIA access key here fails to authenticate, and
# the error SES returns reads like a password problem rather than a wrong-kind-
# of-credential one.
#
# The password is read by the postfix container only. It is never passed to
# the web app, never stored in Postgres and never shown in the UI; it is
# written to /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd at mode 0600 inside that container.
# Setting one of these two without the other stops the container on purpose.
#
# QUOTE THE PASSWORD IF IT CONTAINS A $. Docker Compose interpolates `$name`
# inside an unquoted .env value, so SMTP_RELAY_PASSWORD=ab$cd reaches the
# container as `ab` and the relay answers 535 as if the password were simply
# wrong. Single quotes are taken literally:
#
#   SMTP_RELAY_PASSWORD='ab$cd'
#
# SES's own SMTP passwords are base64 and never contain one; a custom
# provider's token might.
SMTP_RELAY_USERNAME=
SMTP_RELAY_PASSWORD=

# Changing any of the above takes effect on `docker compose up -d postfix`.
#
# With a relay on, UNA stops DKIM-signing outbound mail itself (the s=una
# signature is for direct delivery and would not match what the relay sends),
# so publish the SPF and DKIM records your provider documents. For SES that is
# an SPF include:amazonses.com and the three CNAMEs the SES console shows for
# Easy DKIM. Settings -> Sending says all of this in the app.

NODE_ENV=production

# Which published images to run. Leave these alone unless you are testing a
# pre-release tag or a fork.
IMAGE_TAG=latest
GITHUB_REPOSITORY=roncanfil/una.email
